jennifer lee morgan is the special envoy  for international climate action from  the german government you might know her  as the head of greenpeace international  it's the first time here in washington  in her new role just ahead of the g7  summit jennifer thank you so much for  making time for deutsche welle great to  see you  you started with high hopes to push  forward a climate-oriented foreign  policy  what is left of that in the time of war  there's still a lot left in a time of  war i mean obviously  the russian aggression and working for  peace is a top priority but this war  has taught us how dangerous it is to be  so dependent on fossil fuels  and so  we are working very hard to  phase out of course russian fossil fuels  but also all fossil fuels and to do that  we're accelerating our energy  transformation to renewable energy and  energy efficiency and working around the  world to get others to do the same and  that's a big part of international  climate politics and policy  you here in washington obviously the  biden administration also started really  strong on fighting the  climate catastrophe they even had a  climate tsar or put a climate star  in place how did the war in ukraine  change the whole discussion here in the  united states when it comes to renewable  energy well i think it's it's  interesting i think in the united states  on the one hand  you know as in germany one sees okay  with renewable energy um you're quite  independent actually from others but the  united states  has a lot of fossil fuels  and i think what's happened here is that  instead of you know moving more quickly  i mean the president is trying to move  quickly to renewable energy you see the  very large interests of fossil fuels  working now in this moment of disruption  to try and use it  to get more fossil fuels out of the  ground and build long-term fossil fuel  infrastructure and that's really the  crossroads that we're in right now so do  countries like the united states and  germany and europe we're going in that  direction  move more into  secure renewable energy or do they lock  in long-term fossil fuels and obviously  in washington there's a lot of interests  working to lock in long-term fossil  fuels do you have a conversation with  with government with the government here  i think that the president from  everything i understand remains  very committed on climate change and is  trying to get something done  uh through congress  on climate and renewable energy  but obviously the pressures are huge  it's hugely concerning at the same time  if the united states moves more into  fossil fuel exploration when we know and  scientists tell us that that's actually  what we cannot do  at this moment  it requires just tremendous  leadership  but i also have met with many here civil  society groups think tanks  companies as well  who want to move in and are building the  base of support for a cleaner energy  future  but that's this is this crossroads that  we're in right now but yeah i'm am i  worried absolutely  is there any chance that europe  or germany and europe are getting  prepared  for a united states which is kind of  leaving  the climate commitment again well i  think you know we've been there once  and uh it was uh very detrimental i  think on the one hand  on the global level but i think what we  learned through  the years when  president trump was in office who was a  climate denier was how much we can work  with other actors  so working with mayors working with  those that want to move forward  and having exchanges that are happening  between our countries ahead of the g7  meeting in germany  many people demand that germany and  other rich countries do more to support  the global south in moving  towards renewable energy what can we  expect from germany to do first we need  to all do more we all agreed in glasgow  to  increase our ambition  more renewable energy bringing in  methane other greenhouse gases  so we  pledged to do that and that obviously is  a big signal to other countries  major emerging economies that they need  to do the same  and for developing countries we've  committed to really move forward on our  climate finance to meet the commitments  that we have made which  are still outstanding which is of great  concern to developing countries  but also to double our funding for  adaptation  and to really start engaging on these  issues of loss and damage you know what  happens  what happens when uh an island  loses  its all its home the g7 has committed to  really move forward actively on that  conversation and send the signal that  we're going to stand in solidarity but  that would be my question sending out a  signal is nice talking about it is  wonderful  kind of paying condolences if someone  dies  or hundreds or thousands of people are  dying in floods and droughts all around  the world but what precisely  is going is germany going to suggest  there in the g7  so i think germany  specifically is looking at a few things  so first of all  indeed you know  saying that we are going to be  supporting  countries in their  transition away from coal  or looking to do that um we're in  negotiations with a number of countries  right now from the global south on how  to do that  so i think how we support those  countries is is a topic right now that's  being uh negotiated  i think the other thing uh is how we can  be driving the decarbonization of the  industrial sector so steel cement etc  and the foundation um of interest of a  climate club or a group of countries  that are moving forward not only the g7  countries but offering a hand also  obviously to developing countries that  want to join and and looking to see how  we can support them  uh in doing that  and then really um committing to double  down on these financial commitments so  it's not just a signal it's actually  okay how are we going to meet that 100  billion  uh commitment that was made so long ago  by developed country so this you would  you say is still a top priority for the  german government for the g7 summit yes  absolutely i think the climate issue the  climate crisis is a top priority for the  g7  obviously other issues around food  security uh obviously the the russian  aggression and and how we're working  together to support ukraine uh towards  peace are key issues but climate is  still at that top table  jennifer morgan how does it make you  feel when you see a green energy  minister talking about going back to  coal  and maybe even going back to nuclear  energy it may how it makes me feel  i think i'd start with  how i feel when he talks about  the importance of accelerating the  energy transition the 500 page bill  that's in front of the parliament to do  that all of the very detailed measures  that we can meet an 80 renewable energy  target by 2030  and i think um  i think his honesty about the fact that  this is a bitter pill but that we have  to  basically make sure that we have the  ability to  have  our our citizens  safe and secure moving in the coming  months  is is understandable i think it's  responsible but also you know his  commitment to continue uh our commitment  to phase out coal  uh by you know aiming for that 2030 goal  that makes me feel  as if we are being responsible that we  are sticking with our climate goals  which is one of my main messages here in  the united states  but  that hasn't gone away but that we also  have  we are in an acute extreme situation  that we need to negotiate around and  therefore we have in this short time  period  to look at how we make sure that we are  energy secure how much is the former  government  the american administration to be blamed  for that situation germany's in  well i think certainly we've learned uh  that having such a high dependency uh on  one country  for so much of our energy  is not good policy making uh it has set  up  immense vulnerabilities  so now this government is  working to change that and also to learn  from it that we we just can't be that  that dependent uh we're taking that  responsibility very seriously while also  keeping climate at the top of our agenda  uh but um no it wasn't an easy situation  to come into jennifer morgan you took  the plunge from activism to policy  to a policy maker is this the way to go  from calling  for responsibility to actually taking  responsibility  well i think  i think when an opportunity arises as an  activist and i still consider myself i  call myself i guess an activist diplomat  now  uh to come in and work for someone like  uh minister barabak to make change in  the world  and be a decision maker that's something  that uh an opportunity that you just  have to jump into  and i'm honored to be here i'm trying to  do my best to try and move us forward  and make the decisions that will propel  us into a zero-carbon world last  question what has been the most painful  part from shifting from activism into  politics  when you make a shift like this from  kind of outside  uh being an activist into being a  decision maker i think  i don't know if i would call it painful  but i think that the intensity of the  responsibility  um  is is greater  at greenpeace when i led greenpeace and  worked for other ngos i always felt a  responsibility to our supporters of  course who funded us to do things but as  a member of a government really making  decisions i feel a much greater sense of  responsibility  to the tasks that i've been given and to  the people who i'm you know representing  and that's um  that i carry with me every day  jennifer lee morgan thank you so much  for your time and let's keep this  conversation going we'd love to thank  you so much
 

